# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...but the documented template must be committed: it is how anyone sets the
# project up, and it contains no real values.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# rendered email previews (pnpm preview:emails)
/.email-preview/

# Full-resolution photograph originals (assets/photos).
#
# The derived files in public/images ARE committed, because they are what the
# site and the emails serve. These are the camera files behind them, 5.9MB of
# JPEG that nothing at runtime reads: scripts/build-email-photos.mjs is the only
# consumer and it is run by hand when the photographs change.
#
# They are kept out of the repo rather than in it because git stores every
# version of a binary forever, and a re-crop of nine photographs would add
# another 5.9MB that can never be removed. They live in the programme's own
# Drive alongside the permissions for them, which is also where the next person
# has to go for the ones we were not given.
/assets/photos/
